  "devdoc": {
    "kind": "dev",
    "methods": {
      "constructor": {
        "params": {
          "_implementation": "The V4 implementation new proxies are created over.         Must have code and answer `factory()`.",
          "_legacyImplementations": "The V2 and V3 implementations existing        proxies were built over. Each must have code. They are recorded as        recognisable, not approved: they carry no upgrade gate, which is        exactly why nothing is created over them here.",
          "_protocolSafe": "The only address that may set the implementation or        register a module. Must have code."
        }
      },
      "createModule(address,address[],address[])": {
        "details": "Callable by `safe` itself, or by the protocol safe on its behalf.      Idempotent per safe: a second call returns the module already      recorded, deploys nothing and emits nothing. An adopted module comes      first: if `registerModule` recorded one for this safe, this returns it      and never deploys anything, which is what makes adoption final even      for a safe that has never had a created module."
      },
      "getModulesForSafe(address)": {
        "details": "A view over both halves of the record. The adopted entry comes      first, so the answer agrees with `moduleAddress` and `createModule`."
      },
      "moduleAddress(address)": {
        "details": "Adopted first, created second: `registerModule` is the protocol's      statement and `createModule` is the safe's own, so the protocol's      wins and a create by the publication cannot shadow it. Once either      half is set, `createModule` returns that module, deploys nothing and      emits nothing, so this view cannot drift from what a create would do.      With neither, it is the prediction under `latestImplementation`,      which names a create that has not happened."
      },
      "moduleAddressForVersion(address,uint256)": {
        "details": "1-based over `implementationHistory`; reverts `InvalidVersion`      outside `1..implementationCount()`. This answers for a generation,      not for the module the safe has: only `moduleAddress` follows the      module that exists."
      },
      "registerModule(address,address,address)": {
        "details": "The protocol safe's write, never the publication's: this is the      protocol saying which module is a publication's module, and a      publication's Safe cannot say it about itself.      Zero addresses and a module without code are refused. So is a module      whose `safe()` is not `safe` (`SafeMismatch(safe, moduleSafe)`): the      binding is what stops a module minted for one publication being filed      under another, and it is why a bare implementation address, which has      no storage and answers `safe()` with zero, cannot be registered even      when its address is in the legacy set.      Adoption is final on its own side: registering the same module again      returns quietly, and a different module reverts      `AlreadyRegistered(safe, existing)`. Nothing overwrites it, and      `createModule` returns the adopted module rather than deploying over      it, so a reader that resolved a module once cannot have it swapped      under it.      A module is accepted on either of two facts:        a) it answers `factory()` with this address, and `implementation`           is one this factory approved — a proxy deployed here, whose           upgrades this contract governs; or        b) it does not, and `implementation` is one the constructor           recorded as legacy — a V2 or V3 publication at a plain-CREATE           address it cannot leave, whose proxy was upgraded to V4 in place.      `implementation` is named by the caller because a contract cannot read      another contract's storage: the ERC-1967 slot      (`0x360894a1…382bbc`) is legible over RPC, not from inside this      contract. Branch (b) therefore checks set membership and nothing about      the module's code: a proxy upgraded since, or a contract with no      relation to the implementation named, is accepted if it answers      `safe()`. What the protocol safe relies on is its own slot read before      the proposal, which this contract cannot repeat or verify. Branch (a)      is the only one where the approval set governs the code.      `implementation` in `ModuleRegistered` is the argument, recorded so the      record says which implementation the protocol was told to expect."
      },
      "setImplementation(address)": {
        "details": "Only the protocol safe. Existing proxies are unaffected, and so is      their address: `moduleAddress` moves, `moduleAddressForVersion` does      not. The new implementation also becomes a legal upgrade target for      every V4 module already deployed, without touching any of them."
      }
    },
    "stateVariables": {
      "MODULE_ROLE": {
        "details": "The salt domain. One module kind per factory, so the domain keeps      this address apart from any other CREATE2 deployer in the protocol."
      },
      "isApprovedImplementation": {
        "details": "Written only by the constructor and `setImplementation`, so it holds      every implementation in `implementationHistory` and nothing else."
      },
      "isLegacyImplementation": {
        "details": "Written once, by the constructor. No setter, so the set cannot grow      after deployment and no later call can widen what `registerModule`      recognises."
      },
      "registeredModule": {
        "details": "Written by `registerModule` and by nothing else, once per safe.      Outranks `safeModules` in `moduleAddress`, `getModulesForSafe` and      `createModule`, so adoption is not something a later create can undo."
      },
      "safeModules": {
        "details": "Kept apart from `registeredModule` so a safe that calls      `createModule` cannot occupy the entry the protocol safe is about to      fill. Every read that answers \"which module\" prefers the registered      entry, so a created module never outranks an adopted one."
      }
    },
    "version": 1
  },
